Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is one of the SOLID design principles in object-oriented programming.

➡️ High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions (interfaces).

➡️ Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.

Simple meaning:

Instead of directly creating or depending on concrete classes, classes should depend on interfaces or abstractions. This makes code flexible, testable, and easy to change.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DIP Violation :

class EmailService {
    void send(String msg) {
        System.out.println("Sending EMAIL: " + msg);
    }
}

class NotificationManager {
    private EmailService emailService = new EmailService();

    void notifyUser(String msg) {
        emailService.send(msg);
    }
}

Problems:
    NotificationManager depends directly on EmailService.
    If tomorrow you want SMS → must change the manager.
    Hard to test.


Fix:

Step 1 — Create abstraction (interface)

    interface MessageService {
        void send(String msg);
    }

Step 2 — Low-level modules depend on abstraction

    class EmailService implements MessageService {
        public void send(String msg) {
            System.out.println("Sending EMAIL: " + msg);
        }
    }

    class SmsService implements MessageService {
        public void send(String msg) {
            System.out.println("Sending SMS: " + msg);
        }
    }

Step 3 — High-level module depends on abstraction

    class NotificationManager {
        private MessageService service;

        NotificationManager(MessageService service) {
            this.service = service;
        }

        void notifyUser(String msg) {
            service.send(msg);
        }
    }

Step 4 — Usage (Switch implementation easily)

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MessageService email = new EmailService();
        NotificationManager manager1 =
                new NotificationManager(email);
        manager1.notifyUser("Hello via Email");

        MessageService sms = new SmsService();
        NotificationManager manager2 =
                new NotificationManager(sms);
        manager2.notifyUser("Hello via SMS");
    }
}

High-level module
➡️ NotificationManager

Low-level modules
➡️ EmailService, SmsService

Abstraction
➡️ MessageService

Now:

✔ High-level module does NOT depend on concrete classes
✔ Both depend on abstraction (interface)
✔ Easy to extend (add WhatsAppService)
✔ Easy testing (mock interface)


